00:09:22.437 --> 00:09:26.917
A single late-layer residual stream direction is where we pop the hood on full duplex

00:09:26.917 --> 00:09:30.097
spoken models that must decide at every frame whether to speak.

00:09:30.797 --> 00:09:31.677
The gap is blunt.

00:09:32.317 --> 00:09:36.717
Each set of weights looks like it freezes one conversational style, while the engineering

00:09:36.717 --> 00:09:40.237
says that style is a direction you can scale at generation time.

00:09:40.857 --> 00:09:45.497
Full duplex here means listen and speak can overlap so the model needs a speak or stay

00:09:45.497 --> 00:09:47.137
silent call on every frame.

00:09:47.657 --> 00:09:52.717
That decisive quantity can be read without training or annotation and then scaled during generation.

00:09:52.717 --> 00:09:58.777
Shifting this direction moves the speech ratio from the unsteered 21-30% range to anywhere

00:09:58.777 --> 00:10:02.737
between 6-12% and 42-61%.

00:10:02.737 --> 00:10:07.437
The shift remains consistent across conversations, model weights, and corpora.

00:10:07.957 --> 00:10:11.817
A short reference recording from a target speaker is enough to extract the matching

00:10:11.817 --> 00:10:14.657
direction and transfer that speaker's turn-taking style.

00:10:15.237 --> 00:10:19.817
Swapping in a different reference worsens the match in 93-98% of cases.

00:10:19.817 --> 00:10:24.637
The same direction also anticipates on-sets up to 800 milliseconds ahead.

00:10:25.157 --> 00:10:29.377
That early signal is what downstream systems can use for smoother margin handling.

00:10:30.477 --> 00:10:32.737
Conversational personality is not frozen into weights.

00:10:33.317 --> 00:10:37.757
It lives in a steerable direction that can be calibrated per user at inference time with

00:10:37.757 --> 00:10:42.657
negligible extra cost, and the weights alone will not hold more than one frozen style.

